## Verification Rules

The following rules are in addition to the value ranges and enumerations already enforced
through each data element's `Constraints` attribute in the Data Model above.

- The number of values in a `TimeSeries.values` array shall equal the number of intervals
  defined by the `TimeIntervals` data group referenced by that time series'
  `value_time_intervals` (i.e., the length of `timestamps` if given, or the number of regular
  intervals implied by `regular_interval` and the series' starting and ending times).
- Unless `EndUse.is_custom` is `true`, `EndUse.name` shall match one of the canonical end-use
  category or subcategory names given in the Data Model above, at a nesting position
  consistent with that category's documented parent (e.g., `Heat Pump Supplementary` only
  within a `Space Heating` entry's `subcategories`).

Informative Note: This standard does not include a rule verifying that `consumption` values
reported by two or more `EndUse` or `EnergySource` entries in the same document are
non-overlapping (e.g., that flat and system-nested `Fans` entries do not double-count the same
fan energy), nor that end-use totals sum to a correct building total. This is a documented
convention for data publishers and applications to follow -- see Publishing Rules and
Application Rules -- and not a schema-verifiable guarantee.

## Publishing Rules

- Use one of the canonical end-use category names given in the Data Model above for
  `EndUse.name` whenever a reported end use corresponds to one of those categories; set
  `is_custom` to `true` and choose a descriptive `name` for any end use outside that list
  (e.g., a tool-specific process load).
- When reporting `Fans` energy -- whether as the flat top-level category or nested under
  `Space Heating` or `Space Cooling` -- include a `notes` value describing what slice of the
  building's fan energy that entry covers (e.g., "supply fan energy for the packaged rooftop
  DX units" or "general exhaust and ventilation fans not attributed to a specific system").
  Because placement is a modeling judgment call and the schema does not verify non-overlap
  (see Verification Rules), this is the only way a reader can determine whether two `Fans`
  entries in the same document double-count the same energy.
- When assembling a single document from the output of more than one building performance
  simulation tool, normalize each tool's results onto a common `TimeIntervals` entry where
  their native timesteps agree, or publish a separate `TimeIntervals` entry per native
  timestep and reference the correct one from each `TimeSeries`. Use `is_custom` end uses for
  breakdowns specific to a single tool that do not map onto the canonical categories.
- `EnergySource.name` is free text until a standardized energy source enumeration is
  established for this schema; publishers should adopt that enumeration once available.

## Application Rules

- Do not assume that two `EndUse` entries in the same document -- e.g., a flat `Fans` entry
  and a `Fans` entry nested under `Space Heating` -- report non-overlapping energy. Read each
  entry's `notes` (see Publishing Rules) to determine what it covers before aggregating
  `consumption` values across a document.
- Treat an absent `is_custom` or `is_unregulated` value as `false`, consistent with each data
  element's documented default.
- Applications performing code-compliance review should treat `EndUse` entries with
  `is_unregulated` set to `true` as outside the scope of code-mandated efficiency or power
  allowance checks.
- Resolve a `TimeSeries.value_time_intervals` reference against the `id` of an entry in the
  `time_intervals` array of the enclosing `BuildingPerformanceOutputReport` document.
